      "findings": [
        {
          "severity": "Medium",
          "criterion": "ma_vs_federal_distinctions",
          "location": "orientation[14].text; worked_examples[4].steps[0] and worked_examples[4].resolution; ma_vs_federal[7].text; role_duties[14].duty; drug_examples[7].teaching_point",
          "problem": "The only Massachusetts layer the section puts over 21 CFR 1306.07 is the M.G.L. c. 94C, s. 23(d) supply ceiling, and it says 'Massachusetts adds a quantity ceiling without adding a route'. Meanwhile worked example 5, which ends by asking what a Massachusetts pharmacy may dispense, treats an office practitioner's take-home three-day methadone supply as fitting 1306.07(b), and the methadone drug example says methadone for maintenance or detoxification may be 'dispensed as an initiating supply of not more than three days under paragraph (b)'. The section never tells the candidate that Massachusetts separately restricts a practitioner's dispensing to an ultimate user. M.G.L. c. 94C, s. 9(b) limits a practitioner's dispensing to the quantity necessary for the patient's immediate and proper treatment until a prescription can be filled by a pharmacy, and requires controlled substances needed for treatment to be dispensed by prescription. 105 CMR 700.010(A) says that, other than for immediate administration, a practitioner shall not dispense controlled substances to an ultimate user except sample medications.",
