      "practice_mapping_verdict": "PASS",
      "summary": "Every field of the section was read in full. Each legal proposition was checked against current official text, read on 2026-09-16. Federal text came from eCFR (title 21 up to date as of 2026-09-14): 21 CFR 1306.11(a) and (e)-(g), 1306.12, 1306.05(a), 1306.14(e), 1306.23, 1306.07, 1306.04(c), 1306.13 and 1304.04(h). Massachusetts text came from malegislature.gov and mass.gov: M.G.L. c. 94C, s. 23(a)-(i), s. 18, s. 9 and s. 19D, 105 CMR 700.000, and Policy 2024-05 (adopted 9/5/24). The do-not-fill-before guidance was read directly: the Board of Registration in Medicine guideline (adopted October 22, 2008) and the Board of Pharmacy's December 2025 newsletter. The section quotes and paraphrases all of these accurately. That includes the fax default and its three exceptions with the hospice notation; the five multiple-prescription conditions; dating on the day issued; the pharmacist's earliest-fill bar; the Schedule III-V partial-fill conditions; the s. 23(d) categories, the department's power and the absence of a ceiling for Schedules IV and V; endorsement, separate filing and two-year retention; and the current 1306.07 text, including the dispense-only three-day rule and the 45-day delivery window. The section presents the date-of-issuance reading as guidance, not statute, and reports its effect correctly: the guideline records the Drug Control Program and Board of Pharmacy determination, sets the do-not-fill-before date as the date of issuance, and reports DEA's view that verbal changes are not allowed; the newsletter confirms the same 30-day effect for Massachusetts prescribers. All 21 mapped practice questions can be answered from the section, and none is contradicted by it. One Medium defect remains. The Massachusetts layer over 21 CFR 1306.07(b) is incomplete: the section says Massachusetts adds only a quantity ceiling and presents an office practitioner's take-home three-day methadone supply as lawful, without the Massachusetts practitioner-dispensing limits in M.G.L. c. 94C, s. 9(b) and 105 CMR 700.010(A). There are also four Low items: a decision node that could be read to refuse a patient-requested Schedule II partial fill, which s. 18(d3/4) requires; a node applying s. 23(d) to Schedule IV and V; no nonresident-prescriber qualifier in the 30-day quick-reference items; and an unqualified Schedule III label for acetaminophen-codeine. All can be fixed with targeted edits, so the disposition is MINOR_EDIT."
